{"dull":{"id":"4897d30b-5726-4112-a240-ff3886dd1b26","word":"dull","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to render dull","word":"cegar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to render dull","word":"desafiar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to soften, moderate or blunt","word":"suavizar"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"to become dull","word":"desafiar"}],"pos":"verb","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Years of misuse have dulled the tools.","bold_text_offsets":[[21,27]],"type":"example","id":"e0ca9995-37bf-448f-9cc9-58a76ce3f87a"},{"text":"This […] dulled their swords.","ref":"a. 1627 (date written), Francis [Bacon], “Considerations Touching a Warre with Spaine. […]”, in William Rawley, editor, Certaine Miscellany Works of the Right Honourable Francis Lo. Verulam, Viscount S. Alban. […], London: […] I. Hauiland for Humphrey Robinson, […], published 1629, →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[9,15]],"id":"d7968de9-185d-49ad-98a8-5be418ff8427"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs"],"links":[["blunt","blunt"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive) To render dull; to remove or blunt an edge or something that was sharp."],"glosses":["To render dull; to remove or blunt an edge or something that was sharp."],"tags":["transitive"],"id":"a1fa96f6-caa5-4a1d-b041-c999c703c248"},{"examples":[{"text":"He drinks to dull the pain.","bold_text_offsets":[[13,17]],"type":"example","id":"920b089b-4d02-447f-adb4-45dbfb42a1fc"},{"text":"Those [drugs] she has / Will stupefy and dull the sense a while.","ref":"1611 April (first recorded performance), William Shakespeare, “The Tragedie of Cymbeline”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act I, scene vi]:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[41,45]],"id":"514c70cb-cab1-4fb5-a0e8-81744be30956"},{"text":"Use and custom have so dulled our eyes.","ref":"1850, Richard Chenevix Trench, Notes on the Miracles of Our Lord:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[23,29]],"id":"593d9d93-c0d5-4b7c-b23a-caf587d72e18"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples","English transitive verbs","Quotation templates to be cleaned"],"links":[["moderate","moderate"],["blunt","blunt"],["stupefy","stupefy"]],"raw_glosses":["(transitive) To soften, moderate or blunt; to make dull, stupid, or sluggish; to stupefy."],"glosses":["To soften, moderate or blunt; to make dull, stupid, or sluggish; to stupefy."],"tags":["transitive"],"id":"806d44eb-18c3-46d3-b4e6-52546dcb475b"},{"examples":[{"text":"A razor will dull with use.","bold_text_offsets":[[13,17]],"type":"example","id":"cb79fa48-9fe5-495a-89c9-b45d95af275e"}],"categories":["English intransitive verbs","English terms with usage examples"],"links":[["sharp","sharp"]],"raw_glosses":["(intransitive) To lose a sharp edge; to become dull."],"glosses":["To lose a sharp edge; to become dull."],"tags":["intransitive"],"id":"ca15724b-e3b1-4a43-9500-a1516a804c71"},{"glosses":["To render dim or obscure; to sully; to tarnish."],"id":"e55a787f-976b-4965-88c4-bac8be3348ed"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[{"word":"dullen"}]},"dulcimer":{"id":"da5775f5-7de6-4bb1-b645-6d53a3207357","word":"dulcimer","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"musical instrument","tags":["masculine"],"word":"saltério"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"A damsel with a dulcimer / In a vision once I saw: / It was an Abyssinian maid / And on her dulcimer she played, / Singing of Mount Abora.","ref":"1797, Samuel Taylor Coleridge, “Kubla Khan”, in The Rime of the Ancient Mariner and Other Poems, Courier Dover Publications, published 1992, →ISBN, page 59:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[16,24],[92,100]],"id":"4a877fc7-8fb9-4c6f-85e1-f284fac296b2"},{"text":"Accompanying himself with his dulcimer, a plectrum instrument of his own handicraft, Niles harks back to the balladeers of old.","ref":"1947 January 25, “Album Reviews: The Seven Joys of Mary—John: Jacob Niles (Disc 732)”, in The Billboard, volume 59, number 4, Nielsen Business Media, →ISSN, page 32:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[30,38]],"id":"5aa72923-c7c1-42b6-ba5a-c5f51ae7a79e"},{"text":"Played traditionally, the dulcimer sounds delightful with drones acenting the melody you are playing.","ref":"2004, Madeline MacNeil, You Can Teach Yourself Dulcimer, Mel Bay Publications, →ISBN, Introduction, page 4:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[26,34]],"id":"e3cd1cca-030a-40ab-883d-9a0a8b49b136"}],"links":[["instrument","instrument"],["sounding board","sounding board"],["trapezoid","trapezoid"],["pluck","pluck"],["tap","tap"],["hammer dulcimer","hammer dulcimer"]],"coordinate_terms":[{"word":"zither"}],"categories":["English countable nouns","English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English nouns","English terms derived from Ancient Greek","English terms derived from Latin","English terms derived from Old French","English terms with quotations","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 2 entries","Pages with entries","Terms with Basque translations","Terms with Bulgarian translations","Terms with Catalan translations","Terms with Czech translations","Terms with Danish translations","Terms with Dutch translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with Georgian translations","Terms with German translations","Terms with Greek translations","Terms with Hebrew translations","Terms with Hindi translations","Terms with Hungarian translations","Terms with Irish translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Manx translations","Terms with Marathi translations","Terms with Norwegian translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Romanian translations","Terms with Russian translations","Terms with Serbo-Croatian translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Swahili translations","Terms with Swedish translations","Terms with Turkish translations","Terms with Ukrainian translations","Terms with Vietnamese translations","en:String instruments"],"glosses":["A stringed instrument, with strings stretched across a sounding board, usually trapezoidal, played by plucking on the strings (traditionally with a quill) or by tapping on them (in the case of the hammer dulcimers)."],"id":"38496c6b-5038-4ae3-83f6-56985bcfe1af"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"duly":{"id":"86b1bce5-dccd-4b3c-9f3d-24394178bec9","word":"duly","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"properly","word":"devidamente"}],"pos":"adv","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"The citizen's concern was duly noted in the meeting minutes.","bold_text_offsets":[[26,30]],"type":"example","id":"33f9fe3d-a361-4ee9-8c87-3539a9c777a3"},{"text":"Walcott's display deserved a goal and it duly arrived after 55 minutes. As he had done throughout, the forward ran straight at Chelsea's defence, riding two challenges and even falling before firing an emphatic shot past Cech.","ref":"2011 October 29, Phil McNulty, “Chelsea 3 - 5 Arsenal”, in BBC Sport:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[41,45]],"id":"6d0a80ac-6e33-41d1-b77d-27f82fceee4c"},{"text":"Their first-half display was nowhere near good enough and they were duly punished by Beerensteyn's ruthlessness, with Wiegman also paying for some questionable team selections.","ref":"2023 December 1, Emma Sanders, “England 3-2 Netherlands”, in BBC Sport:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[68,72]],"id":"7dbf59eb-cf40-4731-9c05-bfd81b449524"}],"links":[["due","due"],["fit","fit"],["becoming","becoming"],["properly","properly"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"compliantly"},{"word":"fitly"},{"word":"suitably"},{"word":"accordantly","source":"Thesaurus:conformably"},{"word":"accordingly","source":"Thesaurus:conformably"},{"word":"agreeably","source":"Thesaurus:conformably"},{"word":"appropriately","source":"Thesaurus:conformably"},{"word":"becomingly","source":"Thesaurus:conformably"},{"word":"compliantly","source":"Thesaurus:conformably"},{"word":"conformably","source":"Thesaurus:conformably"},{"word":"consistently","source":"Thesaurus:conformably"},{"word":"duly","source":"Thesaurus:conformably"},{"word":"fitly","source":"Thesaurus:conformably"},{"word":"properly","source":"Thesaurus:conformably"},{"word":"rightly","source":"Thesaurus:conformably"},{"word":"suitably","source":"Thesaurus:conformably"}],"categories":["English terms with quotations","English terms with usage examples"],"glosses":["In a due, fit, or becoming manner; as it ought to be; suitably; properly."],"id":"bf1a3629-7245-4c54-8685-5b219e154b86"},{"examples":[{"text":"The inquest on keeper Davidson was duly held, and at the commencement seemed likely to cause Tony Palliser less anxiety than he had expected.","ref":"1907 January, Harold Bindloss, chapter 4, in The Dust of Conflict, 1st Canadian edition, Toronto, Ont.: McLeod & Allen, →OCLC:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[35,39]],"id":"008548bf-cc44-4eeb-914b-52d88cf38540"}],"links":[["Regularly","regularly"],["time","time"]],"glosses":["Regularly; at the proper time."],"categories":["English terms with quotations"],"id":"e12fcfcd-543b-45f7-a7d2-0960fcd2491a"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"dulce de leche":{"id":"6511b6b8-c14b-454b-b88f-601d9cd951a5","word":"dulce de leche","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"sweet dairy spread","tags":["masculine"],"word":"doce de leite"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Unlike dulce de leche, in which milk is cooked down with sugar until it thickens into gold, the milk stays white here — burfi comes from the Persian word for snow — for a purer flavor.","ref":"2023 May 24, Ligaya Mishan, “The Shortcut to Homemade Milk Candy”, in The New York Times, →ISSN:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[7,21]],"id":"b1b6626e-997f-479c-bee5-8b3fe71d152d"}],"links":[["South American","South American"],["sweet","sweet"],["spread","spread"],["sugar","sugar"],["boiled","boiled"],["milk","milk"]],"categories":["English entries with incorrect language header","English lemmas","English multiword terms","English nouns","English terms borrowed from Spanish","English terms derived from Spanish","English terms with quotations","English unadapted borrowings from Spanish","English uncountable nouns","Entries with translation boxes","Pages with 2 entries","Pages with entries","Terms with Catalan translations","Terms with Esperanto translations","Terms with Finnish translations","Terms with French translations","Terms with Hebrew translations","Terms with Italian translations","Terms with Japanese translations","Terms with Korean translations","Terms with Mandarin translations","Terms with Polish translations","Terms with Portuguese translations","Terms with Spanish translations","Terms with Swedish translations","en:Sweets"],"glosses":["A South American sweet spread made from sugar and boiled milk."],"tags":["uncountable"],"id":"06f60c25-4f82-437d-ad5a-f83fa1358e4b"}],"related":[],"synonyms":[]},"dullard":{"id":"bc459ac7-a352-4dfa-8f92-f92a4ed97b54","word":"dullard","trans":[{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"A stupid person; a fool","tags":["masculine"],"word":"estúpido"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"A stupid person; a fool","tags":["feminine"],"word":"estúpida"},{"lang":"Portuguese","code":"pt","lang_code":"pt","sense":"A stupid person; a fool","tags":["feminine","masculine"],"word":"imbecil"}],"pos":"noun","tags":null,"senses":[{"examples":[{"text":"Oh! Richard of Bury, I sighed, for a sharp stone from your sling to pierce with indignant sarcasm the mental armour of these college dullards.","ref":"1880, William Blades, The Enemies of Books, page 41:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[133,141]],"id":"427ea3aa-a152-42f6-91a2-6ec76c6260a9"},{"text":"Whereas some prodigies develop at an early age, Einstein did not exhibit any great genius as a young child. Some people thought he was a dullard.","ref":"1995, Temple Grandin, Thinking in Pictures: And Other Reports from My Life with Autism, Vintage Books, published 2006, page 211:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[137,144]],"id":"4d082117-886b-447a-a828-8173f874a1ae"},{"text":"Marion has long since stopped listening. \"You dullard,\" she says now she can finally speak, \"I'm your chief of Antimemetics.\"","ref":"2008–2021, qntm, “We Need To Talk About Fifty-Five”, in There Is No Antimemetics Division, →ISBN, page 9:","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[46,53]],"id":"ed16a06a-5d85-404b-a166-63e28c7e4df8"},{"text":"“Okay. What if we send him to a very selective private school so he doesn't have to worry about rubbing shoulders with street tramps and dullards?” “No. We have plenty of good public schools here, and they're free.” “I thought you were doing the accounting. Isn't the nightclub swimming in the trillions?” “No, we need that money to fix the house. And I wouldn't say \"trillions.\" No, definitely not.”","ref":"2022 August 9, Ayo Edebiri & Shana Gohd, “Private School” (3:30 from the start), in What We Do in the Shadows, season 4, episode 5, spoken by Nadja of Antipaxos (Natasia Demetriou):","type":"quotation","bold_text_offsets":[[137,145]],"id":"70d78218-4891-4a83-8cbc-cdb04232d7e1"}],"links":[["stupid","stupid"],["person","person"],["fool","fool"]],"synonyms":[{"word":"alcatote","source":"Thesaurus:fool","tags":["obsolete"]},{"word":"asshat","source":"Thesaurus:fool","tags":["US","vulgar"]},{"word":"arsehole","source":"Thesaurus:fool","tags":["British","vulgar"]},{"word":"asshole","source":"Thesaurus:fool","tags":["US","vulgar"]},{"word":"bayard","source":"Thesaurus:fool","tags":["archaic"]},{"wo